{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310750,"nonce":"XFHNJ+zxHFde8bKdfwzjT5UhIFY=","title":"wasm long-gen corruption ROOT FOUND (session 3, REVISION 3) = the 2^31 SIGNED-i32 POINTER BOUNDARY. Corruption begins EXACTLY when the ...","message":"**Symptom (2026-06-14, user repro):** wasm llama-chat, long generation (~hundreds of tokens), temp\u003e0 (e.g. `... 1000 4096 0.7`): memory stays steady ~2GB then SUDDENLY jumps to 4GB and OOM-HANGS (linear memory never shrinks → unrecoverable). Trap backtrace: `wasm trap: out of bounds memory access` in `string_concat` ← `nue.tokenizer.BPETokenizer.decode` ← `GenerationLoop.generate`.\n\n**Mechanism (fully diagnosed):** a `HaxeString` header's length field gets clobbered by a heap buffer overflow somewhere; the corrupted (huge) length flows into `haxe_string_concat_sret` (runtime-wasm/src/lib.rs:244) `total_len = a_len + b_len`, and `alloc_string_data` is asked for ~2GB. `GenerationLoop.generate:133` re-decodes the FULL `generated` array every token (O(N²) string churn), so the corrupted accumulator/vocab string is hit repeatedly. The \"sudden 2GB\" (not a gradual climb) = a single corrupted-length alloc, NOT the per-token decode leak ([[bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak]], a separate ~2.5MB/tok climb).\n\n**GUARDED, not fixed (commit 26dbf46):** `alloc_string_data` now caps at 256 MiB → returns null on overflow → callers degrade to empty string. Converts the machine-hanging OOM into graceful degradation (truncated output at the corruption point). Rebuild runtime-wasm to apply (`cd runtime-wasm \u0026\u0026 cargo build --release --target wasm32-wasip1-threads`; the rayzor binary re-links it at wasm-compile time, no binary rebuild).\n\n**ROOT-CAUSE PROGRESS (2026-06-14, diagnostic commit e81df5a — instrumented haxe_string_concat_sret to dump both operands' headers at the cap):**\n- The corrupted operand's 12-byte header decodes as **3 consecutive f32 values** (~6.03/1.2/0.69 with Q8, ~5.52/-0.70/-0.425 on F32) = **attention-score / logit scale**. So a FLOAT BUFFER overflowed into a string header — not an integer/pointer/index corruption.\n- The corrupted string is at a **FIXED low address 4408 across runs of DIFFERENT ctx** (Q8 ctx=4096 AND F32 ctx=2048 both hit 4408). So it's NOT a dynamic slot — it's the **interned empty-string constant** that `out = \"\"` aliases at the top of `BPETokenizer.decode`; once its header is clobbered, EVERY subsequent decode's first concat (`\"\" + piece`) reads the huge length and trips. The other operand (the vocab piece) is always clean (len=1).\n- **CONFIRMED PRE-EXISTING / NOT the Q8 work:** the F32 path (no RAYZOR_KV_Q8, no side-module, ctx=2048) corrupts IDENTICALLY at addr 4408 with the same f32-pattern. The Q8 dynamic loader ([[project_wasm_q8_component]]) only lowers memory enough to run long enough to reach the bug. Cleared.\n- **`_rt_free` (runtime-wasm/src/lib.rs:117) is a NO-OP** (\"bump allocator, no reclamation\") → runtime string allocations LEAK and are never reused. This is a SEPARATE contributor to the gradual per-token growth (the O(N²) decode-string churn at GenerationLoop.generate:133 leaks every intermediate) — relevant to the \u003c2GB goal independent of the corruption.\n- **NEXT (decisive):** the overflowing float buffer writes attention-score-scale f32 near low static memory (~4408). Instrument rt_alloc (runtime-wasm:101) to log allocations landing in [4400,4420) + their size, AND bisect the per-token f32 kernels (flash-attn scores `vec![0.0; cache_len]` at kernels.rs:47, rmsnorm, matmul accumulators, rope) — find the one writing past its bound at large cache positions. The flash-attn scores buffer is the prime suspect (f32, cache_len-sized, the score magnitudes match), but its sizing looked correct on first read — check `flash_attn_decode_one_qhead` for an off-by-one / a writer that exceeds cache_len.\n\n**MAJOR REVISION 2026-06-14 (session 2) — earlier framings REFUTED by authoritative watchpoints:**\n\n- **NOT temp-specific.** Greedy temp-0 ALSO corrupts (2 OVFs + panic at 1200 tok). The earlier \"greedy clean at 800 tok\" was a SHORT-RUN FLUKE — corruption first hits ~token 850 (≈74,235 decoded chars), and every \"clean\" discriminator run (host-off 600, condvar 600, no-caller 500, static-bands 500) was simply shorter than 850. **Always test long-gen corruption at ≥1200 tokens.** Fixed seed (42) makes the whole decode deterministic, so \"constant corruption values across runs\" is expected and does NOT imply model constants.\n\n- **The host parallel matmul is EXONERATED as the direct corruptor.** A host-side watchpoint reading the 12 bytes at `base+4408` immediately before and after EVERY `host_parallel_qmatmul` fan-out (wasm_runner.rs) showed **0 changes** across a full corrupting 1200-tok run. The bounded y-write `base + y_data_off + n_idx*4` provably can't reach 4408 with a high `y_data_off`, and a host guard logging any offset `\u003c 0x10000` fired **0 times**. What the host matmul DOES do: change the f32 reduction order vs the guest sequential kernel → host-on and host-off produce DIFFERENT token sequences → the guest bug triggers on host-on's sequence within 1200 tok but not host-off's (host-off greedy 1200 = clean [done]; host-on greedy 1200 = corrupts). So `RAYZOR_WASM_NO_HOST_MATMUL=1` only *masks* it by picking a luckier sequence.\n\n- **NOT the `.max(0)` \u003e2GB pointer truncation.** Found + fixed a REAL latent bug: `host_qmatmul_par`'s FFI decode clamped the 3 wasm address params with `val_i32(p).max(0) as usize`, which would clamp a \u003e2GiB pointer (high bit set) to 0 and make the host write to `base+0..`. Fixed to `as u32 as usize` (zero-extend) — **uncommitted in compiler/src/codegen/wasm_runner.rs**. But this run peaks \u003c2GiB (RSS 1863MB), so no tensor is allocated above 2GiB and the fix produces byte-IDENTICAL corruption → this is a latent defect for bigger models, NOT this bug.\n\n- **NOT `alloc_tensor` returning a low pointer.** A guest detector logging when `alloc_tensor`'s wrapper/data/shape/strides come back `\u003c 0x10000` fired **0 times** (alloc_tensor already guards `data.is_null()`).\n\n**SHARPEST REMAINING DIAGNOSIS:** the 12 bytes at the interned `\"\"` rodata constant (addr 4408) are overwritten by **3 pointer-like words** (e.g. `{1044575124, 1067780846, 3230390934}` ≈ 1.04GB/1.07GB/3.23GB). That shape = a struct write (`Tensor{data,shape,strides}` or `HaxeString{data,len,cap}`) OR **dlmalloc free-list/chunk metadata written to a wild free node that points at 4408**. Since (a) no allocator RETURNS 4408 and (b) no per-token op's *intended* write lands there, the leading theory is **dlmalloc's free list is corrupted by an upstream heap buffer overflow**, and a later `alloc()` writes chunk metadata onto the bad node → smashes `\"\"`. Every subsequent `BPETokenizer.decode`'s first `\"\" + piece` then reads the huge len → OVF → cap degrades to empty (RSS contained to 1863MB, no 4GB hang) but the process still panics (wasmtime `fault.is_none()`).\n\n**REDZONE ALLOCATOR RESULT (2026-06-14, session 2) — HEAP-SAFETY RULED OUT.** Built a full `#[global_allocator]` redzone wrapper on the guest dlmalloc (guard bytes both sides + an ALLOC/FREED state magic) with: dealloc-time guard check, double-free detection, size-mismatch detection (catches the `haxe_string_free` cap-mismatch path), AND a periodic scan of a 256K recent-alloc ring for overflows of NEVER-FREED buffers. Ran greedy 1200: **every detector = 0** (0 overflow live/freed, 0 double-free, 0 size-mismatch) while the 4408 smash STILL happened (same 3-pointer-word signature). So the corruptor is NOT a heap buffer overflow/underflow, double-free, or bad-size free. It writes to a FIXED low address (4408, data segment) that is not adjacent to any heap chunk.\n\n**ROOT CAUSE → STACK/HEAP MEMORY-LAYOUT COLLISION (the documented [[project_wasm_q8_component]] merge bug).** wasm_backend.rs constants: `INITIAL_MEMORY_PAGES=256` (16 MiB), `STACK_POINTER_INIT = 16MiB − 4096` (the Haxe shadow stack starts near the top of the 16 MiB initial memory and grows DOWN), `DATA_SECTION_BASE=4096` (so `\"\"` at 4408 is at the very bottom of data). The guest heap (runtime-wasm's dlmalloc, merged in by wasm_linker.rs) grows UP from low memory, and a long generation grows it to 1.86 GB — straight THROUGH the shadow-stack region. Stack (down from 16 MiB) and heap (up from ~MBs) share an address range → at high memory pressure (~token 850) a shadow-stack spill and a live heap object collide; a clobbered pointer then wild-writes a 12-byte struct (3 pointer words) onto the `\"\"` header at 4408. Explains EVERYTHING: redzone-clean (not a heap-safety bug), fixed low target, content/length-dependent (deeper stacks + more live heap late in a long gen), host-matmul only shifts the sequence/timing, not temp-specific.\n\n**THE FIX (layout reconciliation, the hard part flagged in [[project_wasm_q8_component]]):** the merged module must give the guest dlmalloc a heap region that CANNOT overlap the Haxe shadow stack. Options: (a) start `__heap_base` ABOVE the stack region and reserve [DATA..16MiB-4096] for stack only (a \"heap hole\"), or hand-coordinate `--global-base`; (b) move the Haxe shadow stack to the very top and cap+guard it so the heap never reaches it; (c) the PIC side-module path. NOTE the Haxe stack growing down 16 MiB to literally reach 4408 is implausible — the 4408 write is INDIRECT (collision corrupts a pointer at the ~MB collision zone → wild write to 4408), so the fix is to eliminate the overlap, not just enlarge the stack. SECONDARY (independent, helps memory + reduces stack depth): fix `GenerationLoop.generate:133` O(N²) full-array re-decode (74 KB string rebuilt per token at ~token 850) — see \u003c2GB goal.\n\n**MITIGATION LANDED (commit 2ea4de0, 2026-06-14) — O(N³)→O(N²) decode, delays the collision past normal gen lengths.** `GenerationLoop.generate` re-decoded the WHOLE generated-id list every token (`tokenizer.decode(generated)`), O(N²)/call → O(N³)/gen; that allocation churn was the dominant heap-growth driver feeding the collision. Fix: accumulate raw byte-alphabet pieces in a `StringBuf` (`tokenizer.decodePiece(id)` per token) and `tokenizer.decodeBuffer(buf.toString())` — byte-IDENTICAL output (byteDecode still runs on the full buffer so cross-token codepoints are fine). New `decodePiece`/`decodeBuffer` on the `Tokenizer` interface + `BPETokenizer`. **Re-measure (wasm, Q8 KV, Llama 3.2 1B):** greedy (684-tok EOS) and temp-0.7 (872-tok EOS) prompts that previously smashed 4408 at ~tok 670/850 now complete CLEANLY (STRCONCAT_OVF=0, no panic); greedy peak RSS −23% (1863→1441 MB). Collision DELAYED not eliminated — an unbounded gen can still reach it; the heap/stack layout reconciliation (above) is still the root-cause fix, but normal long-form use is unblocked.\n\n**Tools built this session (reusable, all reverted):** host-side `base+4408` before/after watchpoint in `host_parallel_qmatmul`; guest `alloc_tensor` low-ptr detector; `haxe_string_free` non-heap-ptr probe; full redzone `#[global_allocator]` with double-free/size-mismatch/live-overflow scan + a 0x113C tripwire.\n\n**★ MAJOR REVISION 3 (2026-06-14, session 3) — STACK/HEAP LAYOUT COLLISION REFUTED; ROOT IS THE 2^31 SIGNED-i32 POINTER BOUNDARY.** A clean per-50-token `fmt(123.456)` tripwire (must always == \"123.456\") in a forced 1800-tok run (`RAYZOR_IGNORE_EOS=1`, host-matmul, Q8) pinned the onset EXACTLY at the 2 GiB boundary:\n- token 1200: heap ≈ **2.132 GB (\u003c 2^31)** → tripwire CLEAN.\n- token 1250: `fmt-const = -2142173680` → u32 = **2,152,793,616 = 2.153 GB (\u003e 2^31 = 2.147 GB)** → first corruption. Every subsequent garbage value drifts MONOTONICALLY with the heap top (~410 KB/token), staying in [2.15, 2.41] GB, printed NEGATIVE because the address exceeds i32::MAX. By [done] ALL of startedAt/endNow/elapsed read the same ≥2GB region (this is the source of the user's \"[done] N tokens in -2105359472s\" — a corrupted-pointer value, NOT a timing-arithmetic bug; the token count itself is real).\n- The sharp boundary AT 2^31 (not \"large heap\") is the signed-pointer signature. **`rt_alloc` returns `ptr as i32` (runtime-wasm/src/lib.rs:111)** — negative once the allocation address ≥ 2^31; a downstream signed pointer op then wild-writes (the note's \"3 pointer-like words ≥1GB to 4408\" = a ≥2GB HaxeString/Tensor struct mis-stored to a wrong low address). The shadow stack is pinned at `STACK_POINTER_INIT=0xFFF000` (16 MiB) and the heap is at 2.15 GB — **they NEVER overlap**, so the \"stack/heap layout collision\" theory (REVISION 2, line ~36) is WRONG. The 2ea4de0 O(N²) mitigation only DELAYED onset (850→1250 tok) because slower heap growth reaches 2^31 later — consistent with the 2^31 trigger, not a geometric collision.\n\n**★★ FIX LANDED (session 3, commit 496f1f2 + 9cfc55a) — onset 1250 → past 1800 tokens, negative timing GONE.**\nMeasured the guest heap with a `#[global_allocator]` size+live-by-bucket tracker (temp, reverted): at corruption the wasm heap is **1.67 GB base (\u003e64K bucket, STATIC = model) + ~390 KB/token leak (≤64B small objects)**. The base decomposes as **~0.77 GB redundant GGUF file buffer** (`File.getBytes` whole-file read) **+ ~0.8 GB tensor/QTensor data** (guest-side `alloc_tensor`/`alloc_qtensor`; corrects the earlier \"tensors host-side\" read — the wasm_runner `tensor_handles` host path is NOT the one linked, the runtime-wasm guest tensor fns are). Corruption fires at base+leak ≈ 2^31 → token ≈1225, matching observed 1250.\n- **496f1f2 (weight-base shrink, the big lever):** the whole-file GGUF buffer is never reclaimed on wasm (no scope-exit drop) after every tensor copied its slice out (`qtensor_from_bytes_*`/`tensor_from_bytes_*` memcpy — all verified COPY, so freeing is UAF-safe). New `haxe_bytes_free` (runtime-wasm, frees via the HaxeBytes `cap`) + `Bytes.free()` (haxe-std rayzor.Bytes) + a free call at end of `GGUFLoader.loadModelAndTokenizer` (default-on, `RAYZOR_FREE_GGUF_BYTES=0` opts out). The freed 0.77 GB low-address block returns to dlmalloc and the per-token leak reuses it instead of growing the heap → onset 1250 → ~3300. **Verified clean through 1800 tokens; `[done]` prints correct `284.596s / 6.325 tok/s` instead of the 2^31 overflow.** GOTCHA: a Void `@:native` extern method is lowered by the wasm backend with a DEFAULT i32 result, so the runtime fn must `-\u003e i32` or the wasm-linker stubs it (\"signature mismatch user type 6 / runtime type 0\" → silent no-op). Cross-platform SAFE: native already ships a refcount/view-aware `haxe_bytes_free` (runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs:3482), native llama-chat Paris coherent + no double-free.\n- **9cfc55a (Phase 1 string reclamation):** wasm-only last-use pass frees transient heap strings (`compute_string_free_points` in wasm_backend) — validated (frees fire, no UAF) but A/B showed strings are NOT the leak (≈0 curve effect), so minor on its own. Foundation for general object reclamation.\n- **RESIDUAL (future):** the ~390 KB/token small-object leak (≤64B Haxe objects: per-token Arrays/anon/boxes) is unfixed — onset is pushed to ~3300, not eliminated. Full fix = general guest-object reclamation (extend 9cfc55a's pass to Array/anon/struct, UAF-risky) OR stop the whole-file read (stream tensors). Also the irreducible ~0.8 GB tensor/QTensor base could drop if Q4 weights weren't ALSO copied (the file→QTensor copy duplicates briefly; freeing the file already reclaims the file half).\n\n**Why it reaches 2^31 at all = the UNBOUNDED per-token leak.** The wasm backend emits NO memory-reclamation codegen: `wasm_backend.rs` `Drop` instructions are wasm OPERAND-STACK drops, not heap frees; `_rt_free`(lib.rs:117) and `rayzor_free`(lib.rs:1303) are no-ops; `haxe_string_free`(lib.rs:463) IS real (calls dealloc) but is never emitted at scope-exit. So every per-token string + forward-pass tensor sticks forever (native frees via ownership Drop; wasm = \"Phase 1 bump allocator, no reclamation\"). At ~410 KB/token the heap crosses 2^31 at ~token 1237. This UNIFIES the corruption with [[bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak]] (30 GB native OOM) and the \"\u003c 2 GB\" goal: all three are the same no-reclamation root.\n\n**FIX OPTIONS (corrected):** (A) **wasm memory reclamation** — emit ownership/drop-based free codegen on the wasm path (mirror native MIR Drop) + real `_rt_free`/`rayzor_free` via dealloc (needs a size-prefix or `haxe_string_free`-style typed free). The principled fix; multi-session; fixes corruption + OOM + leak; keeps heap small so 2^31 is never reached. (B) **support full 4 GiB** — audit/fix the signed-pointer site so ≥2^31 addresses work; alone INSUFFICIENT (heap still grows unboundedly → OOMs at 4 GiB) and needs (A) anyway. (C) **loud trap at the 2^31 alloc boundary** — surgical, converts silent corruption → honest failure, no length gain. The layout-reconciliation fixes (heap-hole / move stack / PIC) from REVISION 2 are NOT NEEDED — there is no overlap to reconcile. **NEXT decisive probe (to pin the exact signed op for option B, optional):** instrument the guest at the 2^31 crossing to log which pointer computation first produces a low/wild store address.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_wasm_long_gen_string_oom` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}